  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1251 PM EST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nWe start the period with surface high pressure centered to our east \nand northeast,  and an upper ridge building across the eastern \nCONUS. This will sponsor a dry short term through Tuesday and it \nwill be warm for early November. \n\nThe mid week system still looks to bring a round of precipitation \nprimarily Wednesday and Wednesday night, and the potential for \nmountain wave winds in the typical higher elevation areas Tuesday \nnight into Wednesday night. \n\nA mid/upper level trough over the Plains Tuesday will slide east and \nits axis will be centered over our region Friday.  A cold front will \napproach from the west Wednesday before crossing our area Wednesday \nnight and likely exiting by early Thursday. Moisture will increase \nahead of this system, with rain moving in late Tuesday night and \nWednesday then continuing Wednesday night. Models indicate there \nwill be significant shear available, but convective energy still \nlooks very limited. Ensemble joint probabilities of at least 500 \nJ/kg CAPE and 30kt bulk shear are very low Wednesday (around 10 to \n20%) and primarily across the south. While a few thunderstorms will \nbe possible, the probability of severe weather still looks low. \nEnsembles do show a medium chance (around 40% to 60%) for a half \ninch or more of needed rain across most of the area. Higher amounts \nare more likely across our south and west areas, with lower amounts \nin the northern and central valley areas as downslope winds limit \nrainfall on the front end of the precipitation in these areas. \n\nSpeaking of the downslope winds we will continue to see some gusty \nwinds (mostly sub-advisory) at times in the higher elevations and\nfoothills through Tuesday as southeasterly low level flow \ncontinues, but the set up looks more favorable for significant \nmountain wave enhancement of the winds Tuesday night into \nWednesday night with strong gusty winds possible in the normally \nfavored higher elevation and foothill areas of the east Tennessee \nmountains. Right now it looks like advisory level winds are likely\nto occur in these higher elevation areas primarily Tuesday night \ninto Wednesday night. No wind headlines at this point as a watch \ndoes not look to be warranted, but an advisory will likely be \nneeded as we get closer.\n\nAnother short wave will deepen the mid/upper level trough over our \narea Thursday night into Friday, but will have limited moisture to \nwork with.  We may see some additional showers Thursday into Friday, \nbut precipitation amounts will be on the light side.  \n\nDrier and slightly cooler air will move in for the weekend. Models \nare in poor agreement at the end of the period so uncertainty is \nhigh, but another mid/upper trough may bring showers again by \nMonday.",
